# Must We Bow to the Hat?
Tamil title: தொப்பியை வணங்கவேண்டுமா!

An innocent heart; the habit of earning a living through labour; a physique capable of making the hardest work seem easy; a heart that could endure anything. He was neither an old man nor a youth who waited beside the river to speak with a beauty at dusk and then suffered sorrowful dreams all night. He was a middle-aged man who had assumed family responsibility and fulfilled it gladly: William Tell, son of the mountains.

He was tall and strongly built, with a broad chest, shining eyes, and a smile always playing upon his face. At times he sang like a bird.

That evening William Tell was walking home happily, singing a melody. He had sold a deerskin at a good price in the market, bought what his household needed, and was returning to his cottage at the mountain's foot, where delight awaited him.

Switzerland, a beautiful land encircled by mountains, possessed then, as now, a sweetness born of simplicity and an adornment supplied by nature. Snow covered the summits and green growth filled the foothills. Its homes overflowed with happiness.

The people, rejoicing in the mountain air, cherished the dignity of self-rule. They did not roam in search of the goods of other countries, nor tolerate slavery spreading over their own. "Our country is enough for us. Nature's charm and our labour support us. We will neither commit the great wrong of dominating others nor endure another's dominion over our land." William Tell exemplified these convictions and the character of the Swiss people.

He was a master archer whose aim never failed, and his countrymen admired him. He was equally skilled at steering a vessel safely to shore when the sea raged against it.

He did not seek prizes or honours by displaying these abilities. He laboured and lived. Fish were found in the waters, deer in the forests, fragrance in the mountain wind, and peace at home. William Tell lived happily.

But the country fell into the hands of imperial rulers. Austria's arrogant government became an iron grip. The guileless mountain people could devise no cunning strategy to escape the slavery imposed by Austrian political domination.

"This is injustice! This is tyranny! This is humiliation!" their hearts cried. The mountains seemed to ask, "I rise in such grandeur and beauty; why does the sight of me not give you the power to stand erect?"

Freedom had to be won. The country had to escape the dominator's grasp. Tell told his friends: "This mountain land is a field where courage grows. We must not wander as useless men."

The tyrants thought the people would remain bowed no matter how often they were struck. They imagined mountain folk knew only how to climb hills, swim in pools, hunt in forests, and roll through mountain streams, but not how to enter battle and win.

Was breaking these bonds impossible? Their homeland was ringed by mountains that stood majestic through the fiercest winds. Would its people collapse before this storm of domination? A freedom struggle would surely arise, and victory would surely be theirs.

Perhaps thinking so, William Tell walked home without anxiety, singing.

"You there, stop!" an arrogant voice shouted. An Austrian soldier seized Tell's arm.

"Soldier, why have you ordered me to stop?" Tell asked without discourtesy or fear.

"Why? To teach you sense. Fool, what do you see before you?"

On a tall post hung an Austrian hat, the symbol of that country's rule.

"You walk past without bowing. Are you so proud?"

"Must I bow? To a hat?"

"Do you mock us? Do you not know who we are?"

By then Gessler, the cruel governor of that district, had arrived with his troops. He mocked, "Oh, the famous archer! Look how majestically he stands even after becoming a slave. Do you not know the order, witless savage? Salute the emblem of our authority."

"Bow before a hat, an empty hat?" Tell asked.

Sensing the signs of a heroic storm, people gathered to watch.

"Father!" cried a child. Tell had been considering what lesson to teach the arrogant man, but the cry broke his thought.

"Your son?" Gessler asked insolently as his men dragged the boy forward.

"Sir, do nothing to my son," the father pleaded.

"I shall cause him no harm. If danger comes to the boy, it will come from you," Gessler said cryptically. He ordered the child tied to a tree and an apple placed upon his head.

Tell stood stunned. When paternal love rises, even courage must briefly hide.

Gessler said: "I hear no one in these mountains equals your unfailing archery. I shall test it. Disrespecting our authority deserves death, but today I feel unusually gracious, even toward a rogue like you. I give you a chance to save your life. Take your bow and shoot the fruit. If your aim fails, you die, and the poor boy dies too. If your arrow splits only the apple, I shall praise your skill and spare your life."

What a terrible trial! His beloved son, a child still leaping at play, bound with fruit upon his head. To aim an arrow at it? A hair's deviation would be unbearable even to imagine.

Tell pleaded: "Merciless man, would you make me kill my own son? I shall bring down fruit from the highest branch or strike a running deer or charging beast. But if I see my dear boy tied before me, my heart will tremble. Will the arrow still fly true? My hand may not even hold the bow. Would you write a bloody history in which a son is killed by his father's arrow? Do not command this horror. Do not make a father kill his son and become a great sinner."

Tell asked for justice and mercy; the human beast would not yield. His order remained: your life, or the test of archery.

"Then let my life go. I accept the death sentence," Tell said.

He spoke as any father would. Gessler responded with the arrogance of every despot.

"Ready to die? Excellent. But your lovingly raised son will become an orphan and suffer. Has your hard heart no room for that thought? Mine does. After your death the child must not remain in the world. You may first watch your darling killed, then die peacefully yourself."

Gessler ordered, "The son first, then the father."

Tell knew the villain would carry out the threat. Though dangerous, the trial was now the only way to save his son. He selected two sharp arrows from his quiver, aimed at the apple, and released one while the crowd watched in terror.

"Bravo! Splendid!" Gessler cried, patting Tell's back. The arrow had flown without the slightest deviation, splitting the apple into two and lodging in the tree. The child survived. Tell passed the trial, and his fame as an unfailing archer endured.

The tyrant praised Tell's skill, but not with a pure heart.

"William, why did you take out two sharp arrows?"

Tell did not conceal one thought within while speaking another aloud. "If harm had come to my son, the second arrow would have pierced your heart."

The tyrant raged. "You dared intend my death? I promised to spare your life and will not break that promise, but I will not let you roam free." He ordered Tell seized and thrown in prison so that, he said, the wicked thought might leave the fool.

The fearless archer who had displayed such skill was dragged away and confined aboard the cruel governor's vessel. It departed while the people wept. He had committed no crime, yet suffered because his homeland was enslaved and arrogant rulers could act so cruelly.

A great storm rose as though the Swiss people's passion for freedom itself had taken form, tossing the vessel. Austrian power could suppress an enslaved population, but it could not steer against the storm. Everyone feared the ship would shatter at any moment.

Tell was skilled not only in archery but in piloting through violent weather. Gessler's men said that only he could avert disaster and save them. They were forced to petition the very man they were carrying to torture.

"Untie him and order him to steer," the villain commanded.

For a moment Tell may have thought: let these cruel men who enslaved and humiliated my homeland perish in the storm. Then perhaps he reasoned that destroying only those aboard would not end oppression; other armies would come in crowds. Switzerland must put on the armour of war, turn its passes into camps, drive the tyrants out through its own freedom struggle, and win glory. The hurricane must not be given the honour that belonged to a free people.

Tell therefore steered the storm-struck vessel with his skill. The danger passed, and those aboard rejoiced that they would reach shore. Yet the arrogant rulers were troubled that a slave, one intended for their whip, torture chamber, and prison, had saved them. He was skilled in sailing as well as archery. Could a slave possess so much power? Would leaving such a man alive not be dangerous?

Tell steered not only the vessel but his thoughts. Slavery must be destroyed. Otherwise tyrants would make fathers kill sons and imprison anyone whose ability frightened them. His beautiful homeland, with its mountains, waters, and forests, could no longer remain in arrogant hands. His people possessed both the power to resist and the ability to complete the task successfully; justice stood with them.

As the vessel neared shore, Tell abandoned it, leapt onto a nearby rock, and disappeared into the forest. The vessel began to struggle again.

"Friends, I have returned! Tighten your belts. Let us destroy the murderers' rule. Take whatever lies at hand and rise. Courage to defend the homeland, inherited integrity, and bravery nurtured by the mountains will not be wasted. Cast off despair; the hour of the freedom struggle has come.

"Look at me. Was the danger I faced ordinary? They plotted to make me kill my son with my own hand. They failed. My arrow flew, split the fruit in two, and saved the child. My aim did not deviate. It will be the same in the struggle for national liberation. Our aim will not fail. They dragged me toward the torture chamber; now the storm tortures them. Destruction would be relief for them, but if we raise our own storm demanding freedom, we can prevail. We must make the effort, for it is our homeland that must be rescued."

Whatever words the archer used, he filled the oppressed people with courage. Those who had groaned at cruelty rose in fury; those frightened by repression surged forward. The Austrian army was shattered. Arrows and other weapons became a storm. The army fled defeated, Swiss slavery was pulverised, and the flag of freedom was raised.

Switzerland still stands as an ornament to republican government, admired throughout the world.

Historians say that the account of William Tell in Switzerland's epic of liberation lacks historical standing. Nevertheless, this powerful story continues to inspire Swiss hearts. Even if Tell is regarded as pure imagination, the heart rejoices at an imagination offering so much delight, benefit, and idealism.

Our country's imagination contains many archers and Puranic heroes whose aim never failed. In any one of those stories, do we find patriotism, longing for liberation, or courage to resist tyranny? Can we proudly say that while our motherland was enslaved, an archer's power brought victory in a freedom struggle?

What reward did poor Ekalavya receive for attaining extraordinary skill in archery? He lost his thumb. In this holy land there was a teacher who demanded it as an offering. Switzerland did not turn its archers into Ekalavyas; it made them heroes who won victory in the struggle for freedom.

Those engaged in our own freedom struggle must know many accounts of other countries' liberation wars, whether historically established or products of rich imagination. That is why this note has been presented about William Tell, celebrated as the heroic jewel of Switzerland's struggle for independence.

(*Arappor*, 14 September 1962)

---NOTES---
The source retells the traditional William Tell legend and explicitly states near the close that historians dispute its historical status. `கெஸ்லர்` is rendered by the conventional spelling `Gessler`. The title refers to the Austrian hat placed on a pole as a symbol of authority. The comparison with Ekalavya is preserved as the essay's final contrast between skill suppressed by hierarchy and skill mobilised for national freedom. A source OCR line says `என் மகளைச் சாகடிக்கவா` in the apple episode, though the context and scan narrative concern Tell's son; the Tamil source is not silently altered and the English follows the evident referent.
